{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-058","claim_text":"There is a third type of perception: that of common sensibles (like movement and magnitude) which accompany incidental objects.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","common sensibles","incidental perception"],"rationale":"Definitional claim subdividing perception into a three-type taxonomy; referents are grounded in the 428b quote's 'common attributes' and 'incidental objects' denotations (Perception as the genus, common sensibles as the type being posited, incidental perception as the contrast class with which it co-occurs), while the 'third type' taxonomic framing and the 'like movement and magnitude' parenthetical examples in claim_text are extractor-supplied beyond the quote.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — ontology has English 'common sensibles' plus Greek slugs 'koina' and 'koina aistheta' for the same concept; labeled the English form per §3.2 decision. | PL:object-of-type-canonicalization — claim_text names 'incidental objects' but the claim's argumentative structure concerns the perceptual type's role (accompanying another type), so canonicalized to 'incidental perception' per §3.2 decision."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-007","claim_text":"A point, being simultaneously one and two, is properly said to be divisible.","expected_ontology_nodes":["point","unity","divisible"],"rationale":"Thetic claim about a geometric point's simultaneous unity and divisibility; the three referents (point, unity, divisible) are named in claim_text and grounded directly in the 427a1 quote, each passing the §3.0 node-choice principle (concept-match at correct abstraction level, no richer alternative). The analogical targets of the deployment in 3.2/3.3 (Time, discriminating faculty) are flagged as implicit referents rather than labeled; the predicated 'two' has no canonical node at the correct level of abstraction and is flagged AF4.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"AF4:two (numerical duality) — no ontology node at the correct level of abstraction for the Aristotelian technical concept of ἀριθμός / πλῆθος; 'number' is broader, 'discrete plurality' / 'numbers' / 'plurality' are all too broad and thin (see drift-log 2026-04-22 slug-graveyard entry for the seven-slug research result). | AF5:Time — analogical target at 427a1; the claim is deployed to illuminate how the time of discrimination (a moment) is simultaneously one and two hence divisible, but Time is not named in claim_text. | AF4:discriminating-faculty — no richly-canonical node; 'krisis', 'kritikon', 'to kritikon', 'discrimination', 'judgement' all exist only as thin slugs that are also broader than the Aristotelian τὸ κρῖνον technical sense. | AF5:discriminating-faculty — analogical target at 427a1; the point's one-and-two-hence-divisible structure illuminates how a single discriminating faculty can grasp contraries without losing unity, but the faculty is not named in claim_text."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-033","claim_text":"When we merely imagine something fearful or encouraging, we remain emotionally unaffected, like viewers of a painting.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Emotion"],"rationale":"Empirical observation relating two psychological powers: under mere imagination (without doxastic commitment), no emotional affection is produced. Imagination and Emotion are the referents named in claim_text, each a rich canonical grounded in the 427b22 quote; the painting is an illustrative analogy and not a referent; the implicit contrast with opinion/doxa (the judgmental take-as-true state that does produce emotion, per nearby claim-032) is flagged AF5 rather than labeled under the strict-surface rule.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — ontology has rich canonicals Imagination (φαντασία) and Emotion (πάθος) alongside thin slugs (phantasia; pathos, affection) for the same two concepts; labeled the rich English forms per §3.2 decision. | AF5:opinion — elided contrast supplied by 427b22 context via 'merely imagine' (the contrasted state is taking-something-to-be-fearful, i.e. the doxa/judgment state that does produce emotion per nearby claim-032); held back from expected_ontology_nodes under strict-surface rule. The 'opinion' slot is thin and sits within a 13-slug opinion/doxa/hupolepsis concept-cluster with no rich canonical among them; see drift-log 2026-04-22 for the second slug-graveyard instance counted against the item-007 pattern-watch. | PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical — Fear (rich Heideggerian Furcht) rejected as wrong-context on this Aristotelian opinion/doxa claim; retrospective back-sweep flag added at 062 codification of the §3.2 rule (this case = retrospective observation #1 in the four-instance preventive pattern)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-053","claim_text":"Actual sensation can produce a movement that is necessarily similar in character to the sensation itself.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","Actuality","Movement/Change"],"rationale":"Thetic claim at 428b1 stating a causal relation that functions as part of the definition of phantasia: actual (energized) sensation produces a movement (= phantasia, per the definitional frame established at claim 052) that is necessarily similar in character to the sensation itself. Surface referents in claim_text are Perception (the active sensation as cause), Actuality (the energeia qualifier distinguishing active from potential sensation, load-bearing for the claim), and Movement/Change (the produced kinesis). Imagination (phantasia) is flagged AF5 as the claim-internal implicit referent: the indefinite article 'a movement' functions as definite reference to the specific kind of movement the passage is defining, though phantasia itself is not named in this claim_text.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — three synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug) for the same concept, English form labeled per §3.2 decision. (b) Movement/Change (rich, no-spaces, DA-emphasized def including 'phantasia defined as a kinesis') + Movement / Change (rich, with-spaces, Physics-emphasized def distinguishing kinesis from energeia) + kinesis (thin slug) — two rich canonicals for the same concept; labeled the no-spaces variant because its DA-specific definition matches the 428b1 passage context per §3.0 (rich-preferred-by-context-match). (c) Actuality (rich, ἐνέργεια) + Actuality / Fulfilment (rich, engages energeia-vs-entelecheia refinement) + three thin slugs (energeia, actuality (ἐνεργείᾳ), actuality (ἐντελέχεια)) — labeled Actuality because the claim deploys the energeia-vs-dunamis contrast without engaging the energeia-vs-entelecheia refinement. | AF5:Imagination — claim-internal implicit referent; the indefinite 'a movement' functions as definite reference to phantasia in the definitional context of 428b1 (cf. claim 052: 'imagination is held to be a movement and to be impossible without sensation'). Imagination has a rich canonical (φαντασία) but is not named in claim_text, so flagged AF5 rather than labeled under strict-surface."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-037","claim_text":"If actual imagination were identical to actual sensation, imagination would exist in all animals, but it is not thought to.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Perception","Actuality","brutes"],"rationale":"Dialectical refutation (reductio) at 428a8: if actual imagination and actual sensation were identical, imagination would universally accompany sensation and thus be present in all brutes; the empirical observation that ants, bees, and grubs lack imagination (per nearby claim-038) refutes the antecedent identity thesis. Referents are Imagination and Perception (the two faculties whose identity is being denied), Actuality (the energeia qualifier distinguishing active from potential states, load-bearing for the specific form of the thesis — Aristotle is not refuting identity of the mere capacities), and brutes (the class over which the first consequent universally quantifies; claim_text 'animals' is a paraphrase broadening of Greek θηρία, which specifically means non-rational animals — labeled brutes per §3.0 concept-match, since Aristotle's empirical counter-evidence applies only to non-rational animals). The 'identity' relation operates structurally in the reductio but is not a posited referent and not labeled. No AF5 flag: the reductio's implicit warrant (universal-over-animals inference) and implicit empirical fact are argumentative propositions, not conceptual implicit-referents.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug); (b) Imagination (rich, φαντασία) + phantasia (thin slug); (c) Actuality (rich, ἐνέργεια) + three thin variants (energeia, actuality (ἐνεργείᾳ), actuality (ἐντελέχεια)) — labeled Actuality as at item 053 because the claim deploys the energeia-vs-dunamis contrast without engaging the entelecheia refinement; (d) animals (thin, claim_text surface) + brutes (thin, source-Greek concept θηρία) — labeled brutes per §3.0 concept-match because claim_text 'animals' is a paraphrase-broadening and Aristotle's actual argument targets non-rational animals specifically (ants/bees/grubs per claim-038). Observation: the ontology's 'animal'-prefixed namespace contains 15+ thin-slug entries for related-but-distinct concepts (animal activity, animal cognition, animal soul, animalia imperfecta, etc.) — does NOT count as a slug-graveyard instance because the sibling nodes denote distinct concepts rather than the same concept, but worth noting for upstream ontology curation. | PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical — animal (Heideggerian-context rich canonical) rejected as wrong-context on this Aristotelian θηρία/brutes claim; retrospective back-sweep flag added at 062 codification of the §3.2 rule (this case = retrospective observation #2 in the four-instance preventive pattern)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-039","claim_text":"Sensations are always true, while imaginations are mostly false.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","Imagination","true","false"],"rationale":"Dialectical premise at 428a11 endorsed by Aristotle (per source-record stance) asserting an asymmetric truth-value distribution across two perceptual faculties: sensations are always true, imaginations are mostly false. The four referents (Perception, Imagination, true, false) are all named in claim_text as the subjects and predicates of the parallel contrastive predications. The claim is deployed as a premise in the larger reductio refuting the phantasia=aisthesis identity thesis (per source-record warrant: 'if sensation and imagination have different truth-values, they cannot be identical'), but the identity thesis is passage-level argumentative purpose, not claim-internal content — no AF5 flag here; compare item 053 where phantasia WAS AF5-flagged because the claim was itself a move IN the definition of phantasia.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug); (b) Imagination (rich, φαντασία) + phantasia (thin slug); (c) true (thin, English adjective) + alethes (thin, Greek transliteration) — no Aristotelian rich canonical available for this concept; labeled the English-adjective surface form per §3.2 decision; (d) false (thin, English adjective) + falsehood (thin, English noun) + pseudh (thin, Greek transliteration) — labeled 'false' per surface-match to claim_text adjective and §3.2 English-form preference. Observation — wrong-context rich canonical: the ontology has a rich 'Truth' node but its definition is explicitly Heideggerian (aletheia-as-unconcealment per Being and Time), not Aristotelian correspondence-truth; labeling 'Truth' here would be mis-denotation under §3.0 concept-match. Registered as pattern-watch wrong-context-rich-canonical in drift-log 2026-04-22, with retrospective observations for item 033 (Fear rejected as Heideggerian Furcht) and item 037 (animal rejected as Heideggerian-context slug). | PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical — Truth (rich Heideggerian alētheia/disclosure-of-being canonical) rejected as wrong-context on this Aristotelian phantasia/perception true-or-false claim; original registration case (back-sweep flag added at 062 codification of the §3.2 rule; this case = registration in the four-instance preventive pattern)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-091","claim_text":"The human being's being-in-the-world is fundamentally determined through speaking","expected_ontology_nodes":["Dasein","Being-in-the-world","Discourse"],"rationale":"Thetic claim asserting Heidegger's view that speaking is constitutive of Dasein's fundamental structural whole (being-in-the-world), not an add-on capability. The three referents are all rich Heideggerian canonicals: Dasein (the entity we ourselves are; German 'Dasein'), Being-in-the-world (In-der-Welt-sein, Dasein's basic state), and Discourse (Rede, the third equiprimordial existentiale of disclosedness — more primordial than worldly language). Claim_text uses 'human being' as Heidegger's ordinary-language surface for Dasein and 'speaking' as his ordinary-language surface for Rede; both canonicalize to their rich technical nodes per §3.0 concept-match and §3.2 ontology-synonymy (rich preferred), with the new §3.0 rich-vs-strict tension corollary supporting the default (this claim's deployment engages the Dasein-specific structure of being-in-the-world, not a generic human-being claim). No AF5 for Aristotelian λόγος: the BCAP-wide retrieval of λόγος via Rede is passage-level argumentative purpose, not claim-internal content (cf. item 037's no-AF5 on phantasia=aisthesis identity; contrast item 053's AF5 where the claim was itself one move in the definition of phantasia).","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — two cases: (a) human being (thin) + Dasein (rich, German 'Dasein') — labeled Dasein per §3.2 rich-preferred; (b) speaking (thin) + Discourse (rich, German 'Rede') — labeled Discourse per same rule. First primary_heidegger item; rich Heideggerian canonicals here are concept-correct, see drift-log 2026-04-22 for framework-validity note on the wrong-context-rich-canonical pattern."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-079","claim_text":"For the Greeks, all speaking is speaking to or with others or oneself","expected_ontology_nodes":["Logos"],"rationale":"Exegetical claim asserting Heidegger's reading of the Greek concept of λόγος (λέγειν): Greek speech is inherently relational/addressed — not a neutral declarative act but always speaking-to-someone (others or oneself). Single content-referent: Logos (the Aristotelian rich canonical for the Greek concept being interpreted; labeled over the also-rich λόγος node per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match because Logos's polysemic def covers 'speech' explicitly while λόγος's def is Rhetoric-specific). 'For the Greeks' is attribution-scope rather than a content-referent: the claim's content is about λόγος's structure, not about the Greeks themselves as an object of discussion — Greeks/Greek/ancients slugs exist but are not labeled here under the attribution-scope-not-referent reading (new pattern-watch registered in drift-log 2026-04-22 with 079 as triggering case). The relational addressees the claim's structure requires — addressee-others and reflexive-oneself — both lack correct-level-of-abstraction ontology nodes: the 'others' slug is Heidegger-introduced but at the broader Mitsein-Others scope rather than the claim-specific addressee-others scope (per §3.0 reject-broader-than-claim), and no ontology node exists for the Greek reflexive-addressee concept; both flagged AF4. No AF5 for Being-with/Mitsein: Heidegger's interpretive lens is passage-level argumentative context, not claim-internal implicit referent (cf. item 037, not item 053). Context-sensitive canonicalization: 'speaking' here maps to Logos (exegetical-about-Greeks) rather than Discourse (as in item 091's Dasein-assertive context) — the claim's argumentative mode drives the canonicalization target; registered as a separate pattern-watch in drift-log 2026-04-22.","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — (a) Logos (rich, English-name Aristotelian polysemic) + λόγος (rich, Greek-character Aristotelian, Rhetoric-specific def) for the same Greek concept; labeled Logos per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match. (b) speaking (thin, Heidegger-introduced) is context-sensitive: canonicalizes to Discourse in 091 (Heideggerian-assertive-about-Dasein) but to Logos here (exegetical-about-Greeks); registered as separate pattern-watch in drift-log 2026-04-22. (c) Greeks / Greek / ancients thin-slug cluster for overlapping collective-attribution concepts — not labeled here under the attribution-scope-not-referent reading (new pattern-watch registered). | AF4:others — Heideggerian Mitsein-Others concept exists as thin slug but is broader than the claim-specific addressee-others scope; per §3.0 reject-broader-than-claim, no correct-level-of-abstraction node available. | AF4:oneself — no ontology node for the Greek reflexive-addressee concept; adjacent nodes (Selfhood, self-*) too broad or off-target. Observations: 'For the Greeks' treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent (new pattern-watch, triggering case); 'speaking' context-sensitive canonicalization between Discourse (091) and Logos (079) (new pattern-watch registered). Third instance of rich-rich ontology-synonymy duplication (Logos vs λόγος; cf. Movement/Change and Actuality variants at 053) — governance-threshold count but already covered by §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match corollary; drift-log trace entry only, no new pattern-watch."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-036","claim_text":"What the concept itself is in its conceptuality is not yet visible.","expected_ontology_nodes":["concept","conceptuality","Disclosedness"],"rationale":"Aporetic methodological claim asserting that the essence of a concept (its conceptuality, Begrifflichkeit) has not yet been phenomenologically disclosed. Three referents: concept (thin Heidegger-introduced slug; no richer canonical for the Begriff concept-territory), conceptuality (thin Heidegger-introduced slug; no richer canonical for Begrifflichkeit), and Disclosedness (rich Heideggerian canonical with German Erschlossenheit — the 'there' of Dasein as clearing in which entities can show themselves). Labeled Disclosedness over the thin visibility slug per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match: the surrounding passage constructs an explicit contrast between definitional-explicitness ('the concept becomes explicit') and phenomenological-disclosure ('still... not yet visible'), establishing 'visible' as hermeneutic-disclosure (Erschlossenheit) rather than colloquial epistemic access. The three-step methodological program that immediately follows (originally-seen → primarily-addressed → spoken-into-conceptuality) confirms the disclosure-theoretic register. No AF5: the 'not yet' temporal marker carries a methodological-task implication but no claim-internal implicit referent. No AF4: each surface concept has a matching canonical (either thin-with-no-alternative or rich). No attribution-scope case.","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: visibility (thin, Heidegger-introduced) + Disclosedness (rich, Erschlossenheit) for the hermeneutic-visibility concept the claim deploys; labeled Disclosedness per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match (explicit/disclosure contrast and subsequent phenomenological-program signal technical hermeneutic visibility, not ordinary perceptual visibility). concept and conceptuality are thin slugs without richer canonical alternatives (no Begriff/Begrifflichkeit rich nodes) — labeled directly per §3.0 criterion (a)+(b). Observation: nearby_provenance is empty for this claim (unlike other BCAP items anchored to Aristotelian Bekker numbers) — a methodological aporetic not commenting on a specific Aristotle passage; see drift-log 2026-04-22 for second novel-provenance situation for Heidegger material (first at item 091). Observation: third consecutive accept of technical-upgrade on primary_heidegger material (091 speaking→Discourse; 079 speaking→Logos; 036 visible→Disclosedness) — see drift-log 2026-04-22 heidegger-technical-upgrade-pattern trace."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-066","claim_text":"Proprium is that one thing which is predicated of many things with respect to a necessary quality that lies outside the essential context of genus and species.","expected_ontology_nodes":["proprium","genus","species","Essence"],"rationale":"Definitional claim (mention/reports_neutral) stating the scholastic-Aristotelian definition of proprium as one of the five predicables. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification performed: source record's `quote` field captures only the Latin portion ('Proprium est unum, quod de pluribus praedicatur in quale necessarium'), omitting the English gloss 'lies outside of the essential context of genus and species' that appears in Heidegger's §5(a) text immediately following the Latin. The claim_text combines Latin-translation and immediately-following English gloss, both of which are Heidegger's own text. Under §3.2 paraphrase-distortion criterion (labels-from-quote vs labels-from-claim): the extractor's `faithfulness: partial` flag is warranted by the quote-truncation, but the label set derived from the full Heidegger passage matches the claim_text labels → elaboration, not distortion. No PL:paraphrase-distortion flag. Four referents (conservative set under Option B): proprium (the defined concept), genus and species (contrast classes the definition excludes proprium from), and Essence (rich Aristotelian canonical τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, for 'essential context' — labeled over the thin `essential` slug per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match). Structural components of the definition-form (unity from 'est unum'; necessary quality from 'in quale necessarium') are NOT labeled under the conservative reading — they are constitutive of the predicable-definition genre shared across all five predicables, not distinguishing content of proprium specifically. Predicables, predication, and differentia are passage-level classificatory context, not claim-internal content; no AF5 flag (parallel to items 037 and 079, not 053).","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: essential (adjective-form surface) + Essence (rich, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι) for the essential-context concept; labeled Essence per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match. The other labeled concepts (proprium, genus, species) are thin slugs without richer canonical alternatives. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification: quote-truncation (extractor captured only Latin, not the English gloss that follows in Heidegger's text) warrants the 'partial' flag but not paraphrase-distortion — label set derived from full Heidegger passage matches claim_text labels, so this is elaboration. No PL:paraphrase-distortion flag. First §5.4 activation on a committed item (items 1 through 9 were all supported); see drift-log 2026-04-22 s54-procedure-first-activation trace. Observation: fourth consecutive accept of technical-upgrade on primary_heidegger material (essential→Essence; cf. 091 speaking→Discourse, 079 speaking→Logos, 036 visible→Disclosedness) — pattern-watch now formally registered as heidegger-technical-upgrade-default in drift-log 2026-04-22 with 4-observation pre-population and first-rejection trigger."}
{"claim_id":"claim-papachristou-2013-127","claim_text":"Aristotle characterizes phantasia as a kind of motion (κίνησις) in the soul that cannot exist apart from sensation and is caused by the activity of sensation.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Movement/Change","Soul","Perception"],"rationale":"Papachristou paraphrasing Aristotle's iconic 428b11-13 phantasia-as-motion definition. Four surface referents — phantasia, motion (κίνησις), the soul, sensation — each canonicalize to a rich Aristotelian node: Imagination (rich English canonical for phantasia per §3.2 ontology-synonymy English-form rule, parallel to 033/037/053), Movement/Change (rich no-spaces variant per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match matching 053's choice for the same DA-3.3 definitional cluster; alternates Movement / Change with-spaces, movement thin, kinesis thin), Soul (rich English canonical per §3.2 since claim_text uses English 'the soul' with no Greek-glyph in claim_text; alternates ψυχή rich Greek-glyph and psyche thin transliteration — first tri-form synonymy instance, see drift-log entry), and Perception (rich English canonical for sensation per §3.2; alternate aisthesis thin slug). 'The activity of sensation' is NOT labeled Actuality despite the structural parallel to item 053's 'actual sensation' → Actuality: the energeia/dunamis specification is doctrinal underpinning of phantasia's dependence on actual rather than potential sensation, but 'activity of sensation' is reportorial-descriptive rather than surface-deploying the actual-vs-potential contrast (cf. 053's 'actual' as adjective directly modifying sensation). Flagged AF5:Actuality to record the implicit doctrinal referent under strict-surface labeling. 'Aristotle characterizes' is treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent: the claim's content is about phantasia's structure as Papachristou reports Aristotle, not about Aristotle as an object of discussion (second instance of attribution-scope-not-referent pattern-watch registered at 079; observation logged in drift-log; observational-not-preventive on this case since Aristotle was not a candidate label up for consideration). Genre note: first secondary_phantasia item; the four rich Aristotelian canonicals labeled here all match choices made on primary_aristotle items 058/007/033/053/037/039 for the same concepts — confirms cross-genre framework-validity for the rich-preferred-on-Aristotelian-material rule (drift-log trace with suffixed token rich-preferred-cross-genre-confirmation, parallel to 091's wrong-context-rich-canonical-framework-validity entry but for the inverse case). Tri-form synonymy on Soul vs ψυχή vs psyche is structurally novel relative to 053/079 bi-form duplications and warrants its own pattern-watch candidate (separate drift-log entry).","source":{"author":"Papachristou, Christina","title":"Three Kinds or Grades of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima","year":"2013","slug":"papachristou-2013"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Imagination (rich, English) + phantasia (thin Greek-transliteration) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (parallel to 033/037/053). (b) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (claim_text uses 'sensation' English). (c) Movement/Change (rich, no-spaces, DA-emphasized def) + Movement / Change (rich, with-spaces, Physics-emphasized def) + movement (thin) + kinesis (thin) — two rich + two thin; labeled Movement/Change per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match matching 053's choice for the same DA-3.3 cluster. (d) Soul (rich, English canonical) + ψυχή (rich, Greek-glyph) + psyche (thin transliteration) — first tri-form synonymy instance (cf. bi-form 053 Movement, 053 Actuality, 079 Logos); resolved by collapsing thin first (eliminating psyche), then applying §3.0 between Soul and ψυχή with English-form preference per §3.2 — net pick Soul. Pattern-watch candidate registered separately in drift-log. | AF5:Actuality — the energeia/dunamis specification is doctrinal underpinning of phantasia's dependence on actual rather than potential sensation, but 'activity of sensation' is reportorial-descriptive rather than surface-deploying the actual-vs-potential contrast (distinct from 053 'actual sensation' where 'actual' was adjective directly modifying sensation). Flagged under strict-surface labeling per AF5 slot-semantics. Observations: 'Aristotle characterizes' treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent (second instance of attribution-scope-not-referent pattern-watch registered at 079; observation logged in drift-log; observational-not-preventive — Aristotle is not in this item's `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts`, so the flag records the pattern instance for trigger counting without rejecting a candidate up for consideration; distinguished from the preventive-rejection evidentiary type). Tri-form synonymy on Soul / ψυχή / psyche is the first three-way duplication observed (separate drift-log entry as pattern-watch candidate). First secondary_phantasia item — four rich Aristotelian canonicals matching primary_aristotle choices confirm cross-genre framework-validity (drift-log trace observation — rich-preferred-cross-genre-confirmation, parallel to 091's wrong-context-rich-canonical-framework-validity entry but for the inverse case: rich Aristotelian canonicals correct on both primary and secondary Aristotelian material)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-white-1985-069","claim_text":"Aristotle's analysis of phantasia has implications that extend to among the most significant and original conclusions of his psychology.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination"],"rationale":"Meta-textual evaluation by White locating phantasia analysis within Aristotle's broader De Anima project: the analysis has implications that reach to the most significant conclusions of his psychology (= his investigation of the soul; scholarly-synonym substitution in the paraphrase, see drift-log s32-distortion-criterion-edge-case). The claim is evaluative-meta about the place of phantasia analysis in Aristotle's project, not a substantive claim about phantasia, soul, or psychology-as-discipline. Single labelable referent: Imagination (rich English canonical for phantasia per §3.2 ontology-synonymy English-form rule, parallel to 033/037/053/127). 'his psychology' is meta-disciplinary frame, not a denotable content concept — does not name a substantive co-referent at the correct level of abstraction (psychology slug exists but is Caston-introduced thin-only with no def beyond author-attribution; Soul exists rich but is the subject Aristotle investigates, not 'his psychology' itself, so labeling Soul would read through the paraphrase per §3.2 prohibition). 'implications', 'significant', 'original', 'conclusions' are meta-descriptors. Possessive attribution ('Aristotle's analysis', 'his psychology') registered as new pattern-watch possessive-attribution-scope in drift-log, separate from the verbal/prepositional attribution-scope-not-referent watch (079) to preserve joint-evaluation at item-25; observational-not-preventive on this case since neither Aristotle nor a possessive-owned candidate concept was up for consideration. No PL:paraphrase-distortion despite paraphrase substituting 'his psychology' for source quote's 'investigation of the soul' — scholarly-synonym in Aristotelian context (psychology ≈ peri psychēs), not concept-shift; logged as §3.2 row-refinement candidate in drift-log s32-distortion-criterion-edge-case.","source":{"author":"White, Kevin","title":"The Meaning of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima III, 3-8","year":"1985","slug":"white-1985"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: Imagination (rich, English) + phantasia (thin Greek-transliteration) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (parallel to 033/037/053/127). Observation: possessive-attribution form ('Aristotle's analysis', 'his psychology') is structurally distinct from the prepositional-scope (079: 'For the Greeks') and verbal-attribution (127: 'Aristotle characterizes') forms — the possessor is grammatical owner of the claim's subject, integrating attribution more tightly with the claim's content. Registered as new pattern-watch possessive-attribution-scope (drift-log 2026-04-22), separate from attribution-scope-not-referent watch (079) to preserve joint-evaluation at item-25. Observational-not-preventive: Aristotle is not in `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts` and 'his psychology' / 'Aristotle's analysis' are not candidate labels; possessive structure logged as registration instance rather than rejection of a candidate. Meta-textual claim structure: 'his psychology' functions as meta-disciplinary frame (= scope-marker for what project the analysis belongs to), not as a substantive co-referent denoting a labelable concept; 'implications', 'conclusions', 'significant', 'original' are meta-descriptors. No AF5 — no implicit substantive referent under the rule's slot-semantics; the un-labeled material is meta-textual evaluation, not a concept-implicit-but-not-named in the AF5 sense (cf. 037 distinction: claim-internal implicit referent vs passage-level argumentative content). No PL:paraphrase-distortion despite paraphrase's 'his psychology' for source quote's 'investigation of the soul' — scholarly-synonym substitution in Aristotelian context, not concept-shift; logged as §3.2 criterion-edge-case in drift-log for item-25 refinement consideration."}
{"claim_id":"claim-ogorman-2005-100","claim_text":"Phantasia allows the perceiver to mentally size up what immediately appears by comparing it to other objects not presently perceived.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","appearance"],"rationale":"O'Gorman's interpretive claim about Aristotelian phantasia in the rhetorical context (claim grounded in DA 428b via Frede 1992 p.286; extracted-anchor 1412a from O'Gorman's Rhetoric-III discussion): phantasia enables the perceiver to mentally compare what is immediately apparent to other (non-present) objects. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification performed (second activation; first was 066): source quote reads 'what appears immediately before the eyes (the sun, which looks no bigger than my thumb) can be sized up via phantasia by comparing and contrasting it mentally to other objects'; faithfulness_note explicitly flags that paraphrase added 'not presently perceived' as inference. Verified labels grounded in quote: Imagination (← phantasia ✓) and appearance (← 'what appears immediately before the eyes' ✓). The 'not presently perceived' inference is interpretive narrowing (specifies absent-objects qualifier on the compared-to objects) rather than new-referent introduction — labels-from-quote and labels-from-paraphrase coincide. Ruled elaboration not distortion per §3.2 criterion; no PL:paraphrase-distortion flag (drift-log §5.4-second-activation entry). Two labels (conservative): Imagination (rich English canonical for phantasia per §3.2 ontology-synonymy English-form rule, parallel to 033/037/053/127/069), appearance (thin slug for the φαίνεσθαι concept, 'Concept introduced via Aristotle' — distinct from Perception in Aristotelian usage; appearance specifically denotes the way-things-show-up-for-the-perceiver, related to but not identical with sensory perception; per §3.0 (a)+(b) legitimate label since `appearance` denotes the specific concept 'what immediately appears' at the correct abstraction level and no richer alternative exists). Cognitive-operation aspect ('size up' / 'comparing') NOT labeled despite three thin English slugs available (sizing up / comparative seeing / comparison): (i) §3.2 'English form preferred' does not disambiguate when all candidates are English — multi-English-thin-slug tiebreaker rule absent (drift-log entry queued for item-25); (ii) O'Gorman's own key_concepts cite both 'sizing up' AND 'comparative seeing', so the claim's-own-author heuristic does not pick a unique term; (iii) the cognitive-operation aspect is not load-bearing for the claim's substantive content (the claim is about what phantasia does — generate comparable appearances — not about which named cognitive-operation slug applies). 'Not presently perceived' inference does NOT trigger AF5:Memory — the qualifier is a temporal-property of the compared-to objects, not a Memory-faculty invocation (Memory in ontology is rich, time-orientation-specific to past; inference here doesn't engage that structure). 'The perceiver' is structural placeholder (agent of cognition), not a labelable referent.","source":{"author":"O'Gorman, Ned","title":"Aristotle's Phantasia in the Rhetoric- Lexis, Appearance, and the Epideictic Function of Discourse","year":"2005","slug":"ogorman-2005"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — two cases: (a) Imagination (rich, English) + phantasia (thin Greek-transliteration) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (parallel to 033/037/053/127/069). (b) sizing up (O'Gorman) + comparative seeing (Frede) + comparison (Heidegger) — three thin English slugs from three different authors for the same mental-comparison concept; multi-English-thin-slug sub-case where §3.2 English-form rule does not disambiguate; tiebreaker rule needed (drift-log multi-english-thin-slug-tiebreaker entry for item-25). Cognitive-operation aspect not labeled: O'Gorman's key_concepts cite both sizing up AND comparative seeing (claim's-own-author heuristic does not pick uniquely), and the operation is not load-bearing for the claim's substantive content. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification activated (second activation): paraphrase-added 'not presently perceived' inference ruled elaboration (interpretive narrowing of the compared-to-objects qualifier) not distortion (no new-referent introduction); labels-from-quote = labels-from-paraphrase = [Imagination, appearance]. No PL:paraphrase-distortion. No AF5:Memory — 'not presently perceived' is temporal-property qualifier, not Memory-faculty invocation. `appearance` is thin slug ('Concept introduced via Aristotle') but legitimate per §3.0 (a)+(b); single thin slug not slug-graveyard."}
{"claim_id":"claim-nussbaum-1985-049","claim_text":"Sensory phantasia for Aristotle ascribes potentially motivating content to an animal's perception","expected_ontology_nodes":["sensitive phantasia","Perception","motivation"],"rationale":"Nussbaum's claim that sensory phantasia ascribes potentially motivating content to perception is a specific interpretive thesis — phantasia's content is action-relevant (pursuit-or-avoidance-bearing) rather than generically phenomenal. 'Motivating' is load-bearing for the thesis, not predicate filler (cf. drift-log load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization observation). Three referents: sensitive phantasia (the sub-type Nussbaum specifies; first sub-type-labeling precedent — labeled sub-type-thin rather than umbrella-rich Imagination per §3.0 (a) correct-abstraction-level rule; ontology-synonymy 'sensory' ↔ 'sensitive' surface variation for αἰσθητική), Perception (the faculty receiving the ascription; rich English canonical per §3.2), motivation (the action-relevance content-property; thin slug per §3.0 (a)+(b), no richer alternative — Desire is faculty-level wrong-abstraction). 'an animal's' NOT labeled — universal-instance quantifier (= 'any phantasia-bearing animal'), structural placeholder for the type of perceiver the claim applies to (parallel to 100's 'the perceiver' precedent); also avoids the 037 wrong-context-rich-canonical issue with the Heidegger-introduced `animal` slug, while `animals`/`brutes` Aristotelian alternatives mismatch the singular-indefinite quantifier surface. 'For Aristotle' is prepositional-attribution-scope (individual-author); registered as new pattern-watch prepositional-attribution-scope-individual-author per C2 (separate sub-pattern from 079 collective-prepositional and 069 possessive watches), preserving 079 preventive count at 1 and tracking syntactic-form variations for joint-evaluation at item-25. Source record condition: `quote` field is empty string; `ground` field carries the substantive textual basis ('The animal who phantasizes perceives an object as a thing of a certain sort that could become an object of pursuit or avoidance'). §5.4 partial-faithfulness procedure not activated (faithfulness: supported); data-quality anomaly logged in drift-log (third corpus-schema-variability observation after 036 empty-nearby_provenance and 091 Bekker-on-Heidegger; umbrella cluster entry).","source":{"author":"Nussbaum, Martha","year":1985,"title":"The Role of Phantasia in Aristotle's Explanation of Action","slug":"nussbaum-1985"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — two cases: (a) sensitive phantasia (ontology form, Papachristou-introduced thin) ↔ Nussbaum's surface 'Sensory phantasia' for the same Aristotelian sub-type αἰσθητική; English-English surface variation under §3.2 ontology-synonymy (drift-log surface-variant-synonymy cheap attestation entry). (b) motivation (Frede-introduced thin) is closest match for 'motivating content'; alternatives Desire (rich, wrong-abstraction faculty-level) and appetite (O'Gorman-introduced thin, faculty-level) rejected per §3.0 (a) abstraction-mismatch. Sub-type labeling precedent (first occurrence): claim names sub-type 'Sensory phantasia'; ontology has both umbrella-rich `Imagination` and sub-type-thin `sensitive phantasia` for the same concept-cluster; labeled sub-type only per §3.0 (a) correct-abstraction rule (drift-log sub-type-labeling-precedent entry, item-25 codification candidate as §3.0 corollary on umbrella/sub-type relation). Load-bearing-modifier rule applied: 'potentially motivating' is thesis-specifying (removing it changes what Nussbaum is asserting) rather than predicate-filler — labels include `motivation` (drift-log load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization observation; third precedent after 036 visible→Disclosedness and 053 actual→Actuality). Attribution-scope handling: 'for Aristotle' is prepositional-individual-author form, registered as new pattern-watch prepositional-attribution-scope-individual-author per C2 (separate sub-pattern from 079 collective-prepositional + 069 possessive); preserves 079 preventive-count at 1; observational-not-preventive (Aristotle not in `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts`). 'an animal's perception' — `animal` slug (Heidegger-introduced) not labeled per 037 wrong-context-rich-canonical precedent; `animals` (Aristotle-introduced plural) and `brutes` not labeled because 'an animal' is universal-instance quantifier (parallel to 100's 'the perceiver' structural placeholder). Source record `quote` field empty; `ground` field carries the substantive textual basis — third corpus-schema-variability observation (drift-log source-record-schema-variability-cluster umbrella entry). §5.4 not activated (supported faithfulness). No PL:paraphrase-distortion. No AF5."}
{"claim_id":"claim-frede-1992-138","claim_text":"Phantasiai are sensory images or imprints that persist independently of their original perceptual source.","expected_ontology_nodes":["phantasiai","persistence","Perception"],"rationale":"Frede's identification thesis defining phantasiai: they are sensory images or imprints that persist independently of their perceptual source. Three referents per §3.2 paraphrase-faithfulness rule (label-from-claim_text; quote-vs-paraphrase coincide so §5.4 verifies as elaboration not distortion): phantasiai (the subject; Frede-introduced thin slug; subject-position term in claim_text and technical-Greek-transliteration; preferred over umbrella-rich Imagination per sub-type-labeling-precedent at 049 — Imagination is faculty-level wrong-abstraction, phantasiai are the image-products); persistence (load-bearing thesis-modifier — 'persist independently' specifies Frede's defining feature, removal weakens the thesis; 4th precedent of load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization rule, satisfies the codification-consideration condition pre-specified at 049); Perception (rich English canonical for 'original perceptual source' — the contrast point Frede asserts independence from; parallel to 037 labeling Perception as contrast point in 'Imagination distinct from perception/opinion/knowledge'). NOT labeling: Imagination (wrong abstraction — faculty vs products), sensory images and imprints (synonyms-via-Frede's-identification of phantasiai per PL:ontology-synonymy intra-author multi-thin-slug case; surface-prominence picks subject-position phantasiai), Memory (only in `ground` field 'function in memory, dreams, and thought' — not in claim_text and not implicit in claim_text either, so no AF5). Anaphora: source quote opens 'They are sensory images or imprints' with antecedent phantasiai from prior context; claim_text rewrites to 'Phantasiai are...' for standalone interpretability — NOT AF6 per the decision procedure (phantasiai is in ontology as definite-description substitute, so resolve at label time rather than rewrite-and-flag); extractor's rewrite is benign. §5.4 partial-faithfulness procedure not activated (faithfulness: supported); however, drift-log extractor-truncation-blind-spot-in-s54 entry surfaces a structural limitation: full Frede passage articulates phantasiai as 'motions in the soul caused by sense-perceptions' immediately before the labeled quote, which would yield Movement/Change as a referent on a passage-vs-quote comparison; under §3.2 as currently locked, label-from-claim_text rule excludes Movement/Change because claim_text matches the truncated quote; latent issue logged for item-25 §5.4 extension consideration. Slug-graveyard pattern-watch consideration: Frede phantasiai cluster (phantasiai, phantasma, phantasmata, sensory images, imprints, persistence, after-image — ~7+ Frede-introduced thin slugs with no aliases or cross-linking) has structural shape of slug-graveyard but trigger deferred per ontology-curation framing — the under-representation of Frede's substantive terminological richness is an ontology-curation issue, not a §7.1 pattern-promotion case; count stays at 1 of 2 (drift-log slug-graveyard-frede-cluster entry).","source":{"author":"Frede, Dorothea","title":"The Cognitive Role of Phantasia in Aristotle","year":"1992","slug":"frede-1992"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — two cases: (a) phantasiai (Frede thin) is the labeled term per surface-prominence (subject position) and technical-Greek-transliteration tiebreaker; sensory images (Frede thin) and imprints (Frede thin) are predicates Frede identifies with phantasiai (Frede's identification thesis: phantasiai = sensory images = imprints) — multi-thin-slug-synonymy intra-author sub-case (drift-log multi-thin-slug-synonymy-intra-author entry; distinct from 100's cross-author case because synonymy is explicit-via-claim here vs implicit-via-ontology there). (b) Imagination (rich umbrella) NOT labeled — wrong abstraction (faculty vs phantasiai-as-products) per sub-type/output-labeling-precedent at 049. Anaphora 'they → Phantasiai': source quote opens with pronoun, claim_text rewrites with antecedent; NOT AF6 per decision procedure since phantasiai is in ontology as definite-description substitute (resolve-at-label-time, don't rewrite-and-flag); extractor's rewrite is benign. Load-bearing-modifier rule applied: 'persist independently' is thesis-specifying — labels include `persistence` (4th precedent of load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization rule; 049 entry's codification-consideration condition triggered, 049 entry updated to register fourth case and lock item-25 codification onto agenda). Perception labeled as contrast point (parallel to 037). §5.4 not activated (supported faithfulness); drift-log extractor-truncation-blind-spot-in-s54 entry surfaces a procedural blind-spot in §5.4 (full-passage-vs-quote comparison would yield Movement/Change as referent; current §3.2 label-from-claim_text rule excludes it; item-25 candidate). Slug-graveyard handling: Frede phantasiai cluster has surface shape but trigger deferred per ontology-curation framing (drift-log slug-graveyard-frede-cluster entry); count stays at 1 of 2."}
{"claim_id":"claim-kim-1988-062","claim_text":"Hume argues that if we have no genuine idea of power or efficacy in objects, asserting that efficacy is necessary in causation is pointless.","expected_ontology_nodes":["power","efficacy","causation","idea","necessary connection"],"rationale":"Kim reporting Hume's empiricist critique of causal realism: without a genuine idea of power or efficacy in objects (Hume's empiricist criterion: ideas must derive from impressions), the assertion that efficacy is necessary in causation is meaningless. Five referents per §3.0 + §3.2 + the now-codified PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical rule: power (thin slug White-introduced), efficacy (thin slug Kim-introduced), causation (thin slug Caston-introduced — modern-context node, distinct from rich Aristotelian Cause/aitia which is wrong-context here), idea (thin slug White-introduced — load-bearing for Hume's empiricist epistemological frame: the conditional turns on whether we have an idea of these concepts), necessary connection (thin slug Audi-introduced — load-bearing for Hume's thesis per the load-bearing-modifier rule's 5th precedent: 'necessary connection' is the central concept under dispute in Kim's framing of Hume's celebrated critique; concept-match with 'is necessary in causation' surface, more specific than the broader necessity slug Nussbaum-introduced; surface-match and concept-match converge per multi-english-thin-slug-tiebreaker non-divergent case). Hume NOT labeled — verbal-attribution-scope ('Hume argues'); preventive-rejection instance because Hume IS in ontology as a thin Kim-introduced slug, so attribution-scope-not-referent rule actually rejected an available label (distinguished from 127's observational-not-preventive case where Aristotle was not in candidate metadata; 079 watch preventive count rises from 1 to 2 (Greeks, Hume); 062 added as obs #2 of 079 watch, §7.1 trigger deferred per 069-registered observational-vs-preventive distinction pending threshold-weighting at item-25). Cause (rich Aristotelian aitia — four-cause framework) NOT labeled — wrong-context per now-codified PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical (this is the 062 case in the four-instance pattern that triggered §3.2 codification in this commit). Power/efficacy treated as Hume's cluster of related-but-distinguishable terms (Hume's broader text: 'power, force, energy, efficacy'; source quote: 'power or efficacy' disjunction); not strict synonymy, both labeled. NOT labeling: objects (predicate-locative, not load-bearing), necessity broader form (concept-match converges on more-specific necessary connection), impressions (only in `ground` field 'Hume's empiricist criterion of meaning: ideas must derive from impressions' — not in claim_text), real connection (only in source quote, not claim_text). Use_mention: 'mention' first instance among committed items — Kim mentioning Hume's argument, layered with Hume reporting his own dismissive reply within the mentioned content; drift-log observation use-mention-mention-first-instance, no labeling change since labels reflect concepts engaged in claim_text regardless of stance. Kim-cluster slug-graveyard (Hume, efficacy, impressions, real connection — Kim-introduced thin slugs) parallel to Frede 138 cluster; deferred per ontology-curation framing; reinforces secondary-author ontology-absorption sub-pattern distinct from 007/033 primary-author within-domain proliferation (drift-log slug-graveyard-kim-cluster entry adds primary-vs-secondary-author mechanism distinction to 138 entry).","source":{"author":"Kim, Jaegwon","title":"Explanatory Realism, Causal Realism, and Explanatory Exclusion","year":"1988","slug":"kim-1988"},"genre":"secondary_modern_philosophy","notes":"PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical — Cause (rich Aristotelian aitia, four-cause framework: material/formal/efficient/final) rejected as wrong-context on this Humean modern-empiricist causation claim; thin contemporary slug `causation` (Caston-introduced) labeled instead. This is the 062 case in the four-instance preventive pattern (039 reg + 033/037 retrospective + 062) that triggered §3.2 codification of the rule in this commit; PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical now formal §3.2 row, not pattern-watch. | PL:ontology-synonymy — three synonymy considerations: (a) power (White-introduced thin) + efficacy (Kim-introduced thin) — Hume's near-synonymous cluster ('power, force, energy, efficacy' in broader text; 'power or efficacy' disjunction in source quote); both labeled because the disjunction signals related-but-distinguishable terms not strict synonyms. (b) causation (Caston-introduced thin) preferred over Cause (rich Aristotelian) per PL:wrong-context-rich-canonical (genre-sensitive). (c) necessity (Nussbaum-introduced thin, broader) vs necessary connection (Audi-introduced thin, more-specific) — labeled necessary connection per concept-match + surface-match convergence (multi-english-thin-slug-tiebreaker case where the heuristics A surface-closest and B concept-match converge, no divergence forcing tiebreaker formalization; cf. 100 entry). Observations: Hume NOT labeled (attribution-scope-not-referent verbal form, parallel to 127); PREVENTIVE because Hume is in ontology as thin Kim-introduced slug (rule rejected an available candidate) — distinguished from 127's observational-not-preventive; 079 watch preventive count rises from 1 to 2 (Greeks, Hume); 062 added as obs #2 of 079 watch, §7.1 trigger deferred per 069-registered observational-vs-preventive distinction pending threshold-weighting at item-25. Use_mention: 'mention' first instance — Kim mentioning Hume's argument with layered-mention nuance (Hume reports his own dismissive reply within the mentioned content); drift-log observation, no labeling change. Kim-cluster slug-graveyard: Hume, efficacy, impressions, real connection are Kim-introduced thin slugs with no aliases — same shape as Frede 138 cluster; observed and deferred per ontology-curation framing (drift-log entry adds primary-author-vs-secondary-author distinction). Load-bearing-modifier rule applied: 'necessary' is thesis-specifying — 5th precedent of load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization rule (049 entry updated). §8.1 interim-checkpoint trigger met (CONVENTIONS-edit count crosses 10 with this commit's §3.2 row addition) but deferred per option-B reasoning: items 8 through 15 produced zero CONVENTIONS edits, velocity stabilized completely; trigger fires on technicality, underlying framework-instability signal absent (drift-log s81-interim-checkpoint-trigger-met-but-deferred entry)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-horgan-1993-314","claim_text":"A plausible candidate for the target condition in explaining psychophysical supervenience is that the generalizations of common-sense intentional psychology must be by-and-large true of any creature instantiating mental properties.","expected_ontology_nodes":["psychophysical supervenience","target problem","intentional psychology","generalizations","mental properties","creature"],"rationale":"Horgan's interpretive proposal for the 'target condition' (his term: 'target problem') in the psychophysical supervenience explanation: the truth-of-folk-psychological-generalizations constrains what counts as a satisfier of mental property conditions for any creature with mental properties. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification (third activation): paraphrase incorporates section-level topic ('psychophysical supervenience') and Horgan's framework-term ('target condition'/'target problem') from passage context into the claim's framing. Quote field reads 'a fairly plausible-looking contention is that for any creature that instantiates mental properties, the generalizations of common-sense intentional psychology must be by-and-large true of that creature' — does NOT contain 'target problem' or 'psychophysical supervenience' terms; matched_span includes 'With respect to the target problem' framing. Three §5.4 activation mechanisms now distinguished: 066 quote-truncation, 100 inference-narrowing, 314 contextual-framing-elaboration. PL:paraphrase-distortion NOT flagged: per the 069 scholarly-synonym precedent and 138 §5.4-limitation observation, §3.2's mechanical comparison has known blind spots when paraphrase legitimately incorporates immediate context; treating mechanical reading as authoritative ignores the limitation already documented; §5.4 verification on this item operated on system-inferred matched_span content (drift-log s54-evidence-mode-distinction entry distinguishing passage-image-review verification from matched_span-inference verification), supporting contextual-elaboration reading at adequate confidence. Six referents per §3.0 + §3.2: psychophysical supervenience (Horgan-introduced thin slug; sub-type labeled per 049 sub-type-labeling-precedent — umbrella `supervenience` Audi-introduced wrong-abstraction), target problem (Horgan-introduced thin slug; surface-variant 'target condition' in claim_text → ontology form `target problem` per 049 surface-variant-synonymy precedent), intentional psychology (Horgan-introduced thin slug; alternate `folk psychology` only in warrant field, not labeled), generalizations (Fodor-introduced thin slug; cross-author concept used by Horgan), mental properties (Barnes-introduced thin plural; alternate `mental property` Kim-introduced singular not labeled — claim_text uses plural), creature (Nussbaum-introduced thin slug; universal-quantifier subject of the predication — distinct from 049 'an animal's perception' where quantifier was on possessive instrument; here 'any creature instantiating mental properties' is the subject of universal predication and substantive). Slug-graveyard Horgan-cluster (psychophysical supervenience, target problem, intentional psychology, folk psychology — Horgan-introduced thin slugs) parallel to Frede 138 + Kim 062 secondary-author ontology-absorption sub-pattern; deferred per ontology-curation framing.","source":{"author":"Horgan, Terence","title":"From Supervenience to Superdupervenience-Meeting the Demands of a Material World","year":"1993","slug":"horgan-1993"},"genre":"secondary_modern_philosophy","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — multiple cases: (a) psychophysical supervenience (sub-type, Horgan) + supervenience (umbrella, Audi) — labeled sub-type only per 049 sub-type-labeling-precedent (umbrella wrong-abstraction; second instance of sub-type-labeling-precedent rule, drift-log entry). (b) target problem (Horgan, ontology form) + target condition (claim_text surface) — surface-variant synonymy parallel to 049 sensory↔sensitive; ontology form labeled. (c) intentional psychology (Horgan, claim_text surface) + folk psychology (Horgan, warrant only) — same concept, claim_text form labeled. (d) mental properties (Barnes plural) + mental property (Kim singular) — claim_text uses plural; cross-author plural/singular split. | §5.4 partial-faithfulness third activation: contextual-framing-elaboration mechanism (paraphrase incorporates section-level topic 'psychophysical supervenience' and 'target condition' framing from passage context). PL:paraphrase-distortion SKIPPED per user-reversed lean: 069 scholarly-synonym precedent + 138 §5.4-limitation observation establish §3.2 mechanical comparison's blind spots; §5.4 verification on this item operated on system-inferred matched_span content not passage-image-review (drift-log s54-evidence-mode-distinction entry); contextual-elaboration reading consistent with 069 precedent. Three §5.4 activations now (066 quote-truncation, 100 inference-narrowing, 314 contextual-framing); zero distortion-fires; item-25 candidate to evaluate criterion calibration. | Universal-quantifier creature labeled (subject-position, substantive predication) — distinct from 049 'an animal's perception' where quantifier was on possessive locative instrument; drift-log universal-quantifier-syntactic-role-distinction entry. Sub-type-labeling: psychophysical supervenience over umbrella supervenience — second instance after 049 sensitive phantasia / Imagination; drift-log entry. Slug-graveyard Horgan-cluster (Horgan-introduced thin slugs: psychophysical supervenience, target problem, intentional psychology, folk psychology) — secondary-author ontology-absorption pattern parallel to Frede 138 + Kim 062; deferred per ontology-curation framing (drift-log entry)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-audi-2012-031","claim_text":"The explanatory relations in the in-virtue-of examples are not causal relations.","expected_ontology_nodes":["explanatory relation","in-virtue-of","causal relations"],"rationale":"Audi's subsidiary claim distinguishing the in-virtue-of (grounding) explanatory relations from causal relations: the negation 'are not causal' is the second premise in Audi's broader argument structure that establishes the noncausal relation of determination (= grounding) as the positive entity these explanatory cases involve. Three referents per §3.0 + §3.2: explanatory relation (Kim-introduced thin slug; cross-author concept used by Audi; ontology has only singular form, claim_text uses plural 'explanatory relations' — surface-variant synonymy under §3.2 ontology-synonymy, ontology form labeled), in-virtue-of (Audi-introduced thin slug; hyphenated form in claim_text and ontology; ontology also has 'in virtue of' spaced form Audi-introduced — intra-author orthographic surface-variant under §3.2; hyphenated form labeled per surface-match), causal relations (Chalmers-introduced thin slug; plural form in claim_text and ontology; ontology also has 'causal relation' singular Audi-introduced — cross-author plural/singular split under §3.2; plural form labeled per surface-match, second instance of the cross-author plural/singular pattern after 314 mental properties/mental property). AF5:non-causal relation fired (elided-contrast subtype, fourth item / fifth total AF5 instance after 033 elided-contrast:opinion + 007 analogical-target:Time/discriminating-faculty + 053 definitional-target:Imagination): the negation 'are not causal relations' elides the claim's positive content — the noncausal relation of determination Audi explicitly identifies in his broader argument as what these explanations involve (= grounding). Same elided-contrast subtype as 033 (033's 'merely imagine' elided opinion/doxa; 031's 'not causal' elides non-causal relation). Distinct from paper-level argumentative target (grounding as the broader thesis the claim contributes to but doesn't directly assert) — see drift-log af5-subtype-boundary-elided-vs-paper-level entry for the boundary case. AF5-subtype-proliferation pattern-watch entry updated: total AF5 instances now 5 (≥5), splitting-evaluation criterion (2) MET; deferred to item-25 per general deferral pattern. NOT labeling: causal relation (singular Audi) — surface plural-mismatch; in virtue of (spaced Audi) — surface-variant; grounding — paper topic, not in claim_text; Cause/causation — not surface-matched in claim_text (no wrong-context-rich-canonical fire here since no rich-canonical-vs-thin-slug competition for an actually-named surface); facts (Audi) — only in source quote/ground field; ground/determination (Heidegger thin slugs) — wrong-context, not in claim_text. Audi self-cluster slug-graveyard (in-virtue-of, in virtue of, causal relation, non-causal relation, grounding, facts — Audi-introduced thin slugs): fourth secondary-author cluster after Frede 138, Kim 062, Horgan 314; pattern consistently secondary-author-specific; deferred per ontology-curation framing (138 entry updated).","source":{"author":"Audi, P.","title":"Grounding-Toward a Theory of the In-Virtue-Of Relation","year":"2012","slug":"audi-2012"},"genre":"secondary_modern_philosophy","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — three cases: (a) explanatory relations (claim_text plural) + explanatory relation (Kim-introduced singular ontology form) — surface-variant synonymy, ontology form labeled (parallel to 049 sensory↔sensitive surface-variant). (b) in-virtue-of (hyphenated, Audi, claim_text + ontology) + in virtue of (spaced, Audi, ontology only) — intra-author orthographic surface-variant; hyphenated form labeled per surface-match (drift-log intra-author-hyphenation-surface-variant cheap attestation, first instance). (c) causal relations (Chalmers-introduced plural, claim_text + ontology) + causal relation (Audi-introduced singular, ontology only) — cross-author plural/singular split; plural form labeled per surface-match (drift-log cross-author-plural-singular-synonymy entry, second instance of pattern after 314; n=2 at-attestation, below pattern-watch threshold). | AF5:non-causal relation — elided-contrast subtype (parallel to 033 'merely imagine' eliding opinion/doxa): the negation 'are not causal relations' elides the claim's immediate positive content — what the relations ARE if not causal. In Audi's broader argument, the elided concept is the noncausal relation of determination (= grounding); the claim has implicit positive content the passage establishes. Distinguished from paper-level argumentative target (grounding as broader thesis): elided-contrast applies tightly to the immediate negation-paired concept (non-causal relation), not to the broader theoretical entity downstream in the argument (grounding). See drift-log af5-subtype-boundary-elided-vs-paper-level entry. Fourth item carrying AF5 (033/007/053/031), fifth total AF5 instance (split-evaluation criterion (2) ≥5 met; deferred per general deferral pattern; pattern-watch entry updated). | Sub-type/umbrella both labeled (different syntactic roles): claim names umbrella 'explanatory relations' AND uses sub-type marker 'in-virtue-of examples' as restrictive prepositional clause; not in sub-type/umbrella tension (distinct from 049/314 where claim named only sub-type and umbrella was wrong-abstraction). Audi self-cluster slug-graveyard: fourth secondary-author cluster (138 Frede + 062 Kim + 314 Horgan + 031 Audi); deferred per ontology-curation framing; 138 slug-graveyard entry updated. Use_mention: use; stance: endorses (Audi's own view, contrast with 062's reports_neutral)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-mcdonnell-wildman-2019-115","claim_text":"Denying the closure premise forces a separation between virtual causation and digital causation that is incompatible with genuine virtual digitalism.","expected_ontology_nodes":["virtual causation","digital causation","virtual digitalism","closure"],"rationale":"McDonnell-Wildman's critical claim refuting the closure-denying position within the virtual-digitalism dialectic: denying the closure premise (every causal effect of a virtual event has a digital cause) forces a separation between virtual and digital causation incompatible with genuine virtual digitalism. Four direct labels per §3.0 + §3.2: virtual causation (Chalmers-introduced thin slug, used by McDonnell-Wildman in refutes-stance against Chalmers's view — first genuine §3.2 cross-author-concept-collision case), digital causation (McDonnell-Wildman's own thin slug innovation), virtual digitalism (Chalmers-introduced thin slug — Chalmers's view, McDonnell-Wildman critiquing), closure (McDonnell-Wildman umbrella — labeled for 'closure premise' since the specific premise-naming isn't a separate node and the umbrella covers the broader concept; sub-type-labeling-precedent doesn't apply since only umbrella exists, not umbrella + sub-type-thin). AF5 fires (two implicit referents): (1) AF5:causal closure (McDonnell-Wildman's own author-specific causal-closure-of-the-digital concept; the 'closure premise' implicitly invokes the causal-closure structural framework that bare `closure` doesn't fully capture; better concept-match than `physical closure` Kim because McDonnell-Wildman use causal-closure of the digital not physical). (2) AF5:weak virtual digitalism ('genuine virtual digitalism' surface implies contrast with weak/non-genuine variant; `weak virtual digitalism` IS in ontology as McDonnell-Wildman/Chalmers thin slug, paired with `strong virtual digitalism`; the 'genuine' qualifier elides the weak-vs-strong distinction in the dialectic; elided-contrast subtype, parallel to 031 'not causal' eliding non-causal-relation). PL:cross-author-concept-collision flagged (first genuine §3.2 row activation; deferred status — flag and move on per row's decided-deferred policy): McDonnell-Wildman deploy Chalmers's slugs (virtual causation, virtual digitalism) in refutes-stance critical-claim mode, distinguishable technical sense from Chalmers's positive view (porting-mode vs refuting-mode distinction). PL:paraphrase-distortion SKIPPED per fourth charitable-reading instance (069 scholarly-synonym + 314 contextual-framing + 138 truncation-blind-spot + 115 metaphor-substitution: 'separation' in claim_text vs 'wedge' in source quote — synonymous metaphors for discontinuity, substantive content preserved). NOT labeling: causal closure (AF5 only — not directly in claim_text), physical closure (Kim, not in claim_text), wedge (only in source quote, not claim_text — paraphrase substituted 'separation'), genuine (rhetorical qualifier, not load-bearing modifier in the 049/036/053/138/062 sense — distinguishes real-virtual-digitalism from non-genuine variants but doesn't itself denote a labelable concept; 'weak virtual digitalism' is the implicit contrast captured via AF5), virtual / virtual reality (claim_text uses compounds, bare-form labels redundant), Cause/causation (rich Aristotelian / generic — not surface-matched), fictional/fiction (paper title alternative thesis, not in claim_text). McDonnell-Wildman self-cluster slug-graveyard (closure, digital causation, wedge, causal closure — McDonnell-Wildman-introduced thin slugs): fifth secondary-author cluster after Frede 138, Kim 062, Horgan 314, Audi 031; pattern uniform across all 5 authors; deferred per ontology-curation framing.","source":{"author":"McDonnell, Neil and Wildman, Nathan","title":"Virtual Reality- Digital or Fictional","year":"2019","slug":"mcdonnell-wildman-2019"},"genre":"secondary_modern_philosophy","notes":"PL:cross-author-concept-collision — first genuine §3.2 row activation: McDonnell-Wildman deploy Chalmers-introduced slugs (virtual causation, virtual digitalism) in refutes-stance critical-claim mode, distinguishable technical sense from Chalmers's positive view. Per §3.2 row's decided-deferred policy ('flag and move on'): no convention modification, the PL flag surfaces the collision for upstream tracking. Refines the §3.2 row's scope: porting-mode (using prior author's term as-is, e.g., Kim's `explanatory relation` used by Audi at 031) vs refuting-mode (deploying prior author's term while critiquing the prior author's view, as here) — the collision-as-pattern is more salient in refuting-mode. Drift-log cross-author-concept-collision-first-instance entry. | AF5:causal closure — elided implicit referent (Kim-Wildman framework specificity): 'the closure premise' in claim_text invokes the causal-closure structural framework McDonnell-Wildman are operating within; bare `closure` umbrella labeled but the specific causal-closure mechanism is implicit. Concept-match: `causal closure` (McDonnell-Wildman) over `physical closure` (Kim) — McDonnell-Wildman's argument is about closure-of-the-digital not closure-of-the-physical, their own author-specific thin slug fits better. | AF5:weak virtual digitalism — elided-contrast subtype (parallel to 031 elided-contrast:non-causal-relation and 033 elided-contrast:opinion): 'genuine virtual digitalism' surface elides the contrast with `weak virtual digitalism` (in ontology as thin slug, paired with `strong virtual digitalism` — McDonnell-Wildman's own dialectical structure). The 'genuine' qualifier signals there is a non-genuine variant; the implicit positive content is the weak/strong distinction in the broader paper. Sixth total AF5 instance / fifth item carrying AF5 (033/007/053/031/115 — wait, the 115 case adds two AF5 fires this single item): elided-contrast subtype count rises further. AF5-subtype-proliferation pattern-watch entry update needed. | Sub-type/umbrella relation: closure umbrella labeled (only umbrella in ontology for closure-related sub-concepts here; closure premise specific naming is not a separate slug, AF4 not fired since umbrella covers it). 'Genuine' qualifier NOT labeled — rhetorical-emphasis distinguishing real-vs-non-genuine variants; elided contrast (weak virtual digitalism) is captured via AF5:weak virtual digitalism not a separate label. McDonnell-Wildman self-cluster slug-graveyard fifth secondary-author cluster, deferred per ontology-curation framing (drift-log slug-graveyard-mcdonnell-wildman-cluster entry; 138 entry chain extended). Stance: refutes (first committed item with refutes-stance — third stance value seen after endorses-default and 062 reports_neutral; drift-log stance-refutes-first-instance entry). Use_mention: use. Notable absence of PL:ontology-synonymy this item — first item among committed without any synonymy case (drift-log notable-absence-pl-ontology-synonymy entry, negative-evidence tracking)."}
